{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>The blessings of your father have prevailed over the blessings of my parents.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): my parents are Abraham and Isaac, and he says: my blessings shall be added upon the blessings of my parents." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'the blessings of your father have prevailed' — that is, the emanation of Atzilut of your father shall receive and be drawn down from above and prevail over 'the blessings of my parents,' which are the supernal fathers, and they are the attributes in which Abraham and Isaac held fast — and they are חסד and פחד, and they are 'the hands of the Mighty One of Jacob' (אביר יעקב) which I explained to you above. And he called them 'parents' (הורי), for the patriarchs are themselves the Chariot. And so David the king, peace be upon him, called them 'mountains,' as he said (Psalms 121): ״אשא עיני אל ההרים״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'I lift my eyes to the mountains' — these are the patriarchs, that is, the supernal ones. <b>Unto the desire of the everlasting hills</b> — until the attributes reach perfection, and then the Name and the Throne will be complete; and understand this. For the 'parents' (הורי) he mentioned are the very 'everlasting hills' (גבעות עולם), meaning a shield for the world, from the language of (Exodus 28): ״ומגבעות תעשה להם״ — for the entire world depends upon them, and they are 'the arms of the world' (זרועות עולם), like the amulet that the mighty man bears on his arm: the mighty man bears the amulet and the amulet bears the mighty man and sustains him. And so you find that David called them 'mountains and hills,' as he said (Psalms 72): ״ישאו הרים שלום לעם וגבעות בצדקה״. And open the eyes of your intellect and see how Jacob called them 'everlasting hills' (גבעות עולם) and Moses our master 'everlasting hills' (גבעות עולם) and David the king 'gates of the world' (פתחי עולם) — seeing that he already mentioned (Psalms 24): ״שאו שערים ראשיכם״ — which are the supernal attributes, for 'gates' (שערים) is from the language of measure (מדה) and reckoning (שעור). And understand this."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {1 "The last two references: 'and Moses our master גבעות עולם' likely alludes to Deuteronomy 33:15; 'David the king פתחי עולם' — no verse containing the exact phrase פתחי עולם is readily identified; the prooftext cited (Psalms 24:7) contains שערים but not פתחי עולם. The connection is apparently interpretive rather than verbatim quotation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:21:49.373403595Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}